\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The canonical infinite-volume state and its moment topology}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{For #{m_0,\lambda>0} and any increasing #{\ell_j\to\infty},
the states of the \vocabk{continuum half-Dirichlet laws}{fdip-00I2} converge
to a state #{\omega} represented by a probability measure
#{\nu} on real tempered distributions, independent of the square
exhaustion. Write #{J(f)=\omega(e^{i\Phi(f)})} and
#{S_r(f_1,\ldots,f_r)=\omega(\Phi(f_1)\cdots\Phi(f_r))}.
The measure is Euclidean invariant and even under #{\phi\mapsto-\phi}.
The functional is entire on every finite-dimensional complex test
span, and convergence of the bounded-region functionals is locally
uniform on every such span. For fixed Schwartz tests,
##{S_r(f_1,\ldots,f_r)=
 \lim_{j\to\infty}\lim_{\substack{N\to\infty\\N\text{ odd}}}
 \omega_{\ell_j,N}\bigl(\Phi_\epsilon(f_1)\cdots\Phi_\epsilon(f_r)\bigr),
 \quad\epsilon=\frac{\ell_j}{N+1}.}
Put
##{\begin{aligned}
n_H(h)&=\left(\int_\mathbb R(2+x^2)|h(x)|^2dx\right)^{1/2},\\
G_H(f)&=\int_\mathbb R n_H(f(\cdot,t))dt
                     +\sup_t n_H(f(\cdot,t)).
\end{aligned}}
There is a constant #{D}, depending on the fixed model, such that
##{|S_r(f_1,\ldots,f_r)|\le D^r\sqrt{r!}\prod_{k=1}^rG_H(f_k).}
Thus the moment forms are jointly continuous and tempered.}

\proof{
\p{The exact source law is
\citet{(3.79)-(3.81), pp. 293-294}{frohlich1974schwingerI}:
the covariance is #{(-\Delta_{\Lambda,D}+m_0^2I)^{-1}}, the
Gaussian exponent has its canonical factor #{1/2}, and the normalized
interaction is the negative exponential of the whole-space Wick
polynomial #{P(u)=\lambda u^4/24}. There is zero external field.
The square closure has continuous piecewise smooth boundary and is
the closure of its interior, satisfying that paper's regularity
condition. The polynomial satisfies (3.69) and condition (C2), (4.4),
p. 296: its leading coefficient is positive and its linear coefficient
is zero, which the printed inequality permits. The separate
cluster-expansion condition (C1) is not used.}

\p{The norm #{n_H} is exactly (3.58), p. 289, of
\citek{frohlich1974schwingerI}. The equivalent operator-bound norms
around (3.40)-(3.42), p. 286, lie between #{n_H} and a
model-dependent multiple of it; enlarging constants therefore transfers
their estimates. For the weight-one norm
#{n_1(h)^2=\int(1+x^2)|h(x)|^2dx} and its analogous #{G_1},
##{n_1\le n_H\le\sqrt2\,n_1,\qquad
   G_1\le G_H\le\sqrt2\,G_1.}
The norms are not identified with one another. The spacetime norm is
finite and continuous on Schwartz space. Absolute values preserve
#{n_H} and #{G_H} in their function-space completion; smooth compact
approximation justifies the absolute-test inequalities there.}

\p{By \citet{Theorem 3.13', p. 294}{frohlich1974schwingerI},
every moment smeared with nonnegative compact tests is nonnegative
and increasing under inclusion of regular domains. Odd moments vanish
by even symmetry. Corollary 3.14' transfers the entire-functional and
norm estimates of Section 3.4, particularly the first assertion of
Theorem 3.12, uniformly to the bounded-region laws. For fixed real
tests #{f_1,\ldots,f_s}, put
#{F_\Lambda(z)=J_\Lambda(\sum_{k=1}^sz_kf_k)}. There are
#{A,B}, independent of #{\Lambda}, with
##{|F_\Lambda(z)|\le A\exp\left[
 B\,G_H\left(\sum_{k=1}^s|\operatorname{Im}z_k|\,|f_k|\right)^2\right].}
This bounds the family locally on every complex test span. The same
estimates, or Cauchy differentiation as in Theorem 4.2(II), give
#{D^r\sqrt{r!}\prod_kG_H(f_k)} for the moments uniformly in
the region. Consequently the monotone nonnegative moments have finite
limits. Each real compact smooth test is a difference of two nonnegative
compact smooth tests. Multilinear expansion extends the limits to
signed tests, independently of the decomposition, since each
finite-domain functional is multilinear.}

\p{To identify this limit with the precise state of Section 4.1 of
\citek{frohlich1974schwingerI}, put
#{R(a,b)=[-a,a]\times[-b,b]} and
#{\Sigma_a=[-a,a]\times\mathbb R}. For fixed nonnegative compact
tests let #{M(a,b)} be their half-Dirichlet moment on #{R(a,b)}.
The compact-region and strip construction in Section 3.5,
pp. 293-295, removes the time boundary and gives
##{M_{\mathrm{strip}}(a)=\sup_b M(a,b).}
The transfer statement on p. 295 identifies the notation used in
Section 4 with these strip measures. For #{q_j=\ell_j/2},
##{\begin{aligned}
\lim_jM(q_j,q_j)
 &=\sup_{a,b}M(a,b)\\
 &=\sup_a\sup_bM(a,b)
  =\lim_{a\to\infty}M_{\mathrm{strip}}(a).
\end{aligned}}
Every bounded rectangle lies in a sufficiently large centered square,
and each square is itself a rectangle. Every bounded regular domain
also lies in such a square. These facts prove the equalities of
directed suprema; they apply after the ultraviolet limit, with no
interchange of lattice and volume limits. Multilinear decomposition
transfers equality to signed compact tests.}

\p{Theorem 4.1(II), p. 296, of
\citek{frohlich1974schwingerI} identifies this strip limit as its
Euclidean-invariant half-Dirichlet state. Theorem 4.2(I)-(II),
pp. 296-297, supplies its continuous entire functional and tempered
moments. Invariance therefore belongs to the square-limit state as
well.}

\p{Moment equality implies functional equality here through the
uniform entire bounds. Cauchy estimates on a larger polydisc bound
the tails of the multivariate Taylor expansions uniformly on compact
coefficient sets. The coefficient limits therefore give locally uniform
convergence of #{F_\Lambda}. Equivalently, normal-family compactness
and uniqueness of the Taylor coefficients give the same conclusion.
Cauchy's formula passes every mixed derivative to the limit. Compact
tests approximate each Schwartz test in #{G_H}, and the uniform
#{G_H}-continuity extends both equality and convergence. Theorem 4.2
identifies #{J} as the Fourier transform of a unique probability
measure on real tempered distributions. Its finite-dimensional entire
Laplace transforms also give moment determinacy.}

\p{For the inner ultraviolet limit of a fixed Schwartz test, multiply
it by a compact smooth cutoff equal to one near the closed square.
This changes neither the lattice smear nor the bounded-region field,
so \ref{fdip-00I4} applies exactly. The uniform #{G_H} estimates then
give the outer limit for the original tests. All tests, including any
translation parameter used subsequently, are fixed during both limits.}
}
